3. Add a dedicated Charge release clip/state

Represent the voluntary release cleanly instead of hacking the animator index.

Add an animation key conceptually similar to:

chargeRelease: {
  frames: [`${BASE_0001}charge1.png`],
  fps: CHARGE_FPS,
  loop: false,
  heightRatio: 1,
}

This is intentionally the same artwork as the first Charge startup frame.

Use a short logical state such as:

chargeRelease

The state should last exactly one frame-time at CHARGE_FPS.

The current Charge rate is 10 FPS, so this naturally means roughly:

0.1 seconds

Do not introduce a random hard-coded timeout disconnected from the animation rate.

Prefer:

this.chargeReleaseDuration = sprites.duration('chargeRelease');

or equivalent.

4. “Voluntary release” is important

Only play chargeRelease when the player stops holding the Charge control normally.

Example:

player is actively charging
→ Charge key/button becomes released
→ chargeRelease
→ normal Idle/Run/etc.

Do not force the release pose when Charge is interrupted by another gameplay state.

These transitions should remain immediate:

Charge → Hitstun
Charge → BA1
Charge → BA2
Charge → Jump
Charge → Defense/Dodge
Charge → airborne state

For example, if #0001 is charging and gets hit:

chargea → Hurt

not:

chargea → charge1 → Hurt

Likewise:

Charge + BA1 → BA1 immediately
Charge + Defense → Dodge immediately
Charge + Jump → Jump immediately

The release pose represents the character letting go of Charge, not every possible way the Charge state can end.

5. Detect the release transition explicitly

Track whether #0001 was actually charging on the previous simulation step.

Do not infer voluntary release merely from:

input.charge === false

because Charge can be inactive for many other reasons.

Conceptually distinguish:

wasCharging = true
input.charge = false
no higher-priority interruption started

from:

wasCharging = true
input.charge = true
attack begins

and:

wasCharging = true
input.charge = true
hitstun begins

Only the first case starts chargeRelease.

A simple boolean or short release timer is fine as long as the state transition is explicit and deterministic.

